{"schemaVersion":1,"id":"b934861232fa69f0943bde71384ce0b8b1d08d0624d1a471849868fbd081ec82","url":"http://www.bing.com/news/apiclick.aspx?ref=FexRss&aid=&tid=6a9d504070f84c39ac4b3b82f018d06e&url=https%3a%2f%2fwww.rappler.com%2ftechnology%2fmaria-ressa-journalists-protect-information-integrity-nobel-peace-conference-2026%2f&c=17784057799643982011&mkt=en-us","title":"Maria Ressa: Journalists must build, own technology to protect information integrity","author":"Jodesz Gavilan","publishedAt":"2026-09-06T06:30:00+00:00","retrievedAt":"2026-09-06T11:37:33.967Z","text":"TRUTH. Rappler CEO and Nobel laureate Maria Ressa speaks at the Nobel Peace Conference 2026 about restoring information integrity.\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\tScreenshot from Nobel Peace Center\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\n\t\n\tNobel Peace Prize laureate Maria Ressa   says journalists must have a say in the systems that increasingly determine how news is distributed\n\t\t\t\n\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tAT A GLANCE\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tMaria Ressa emphasized the need for journalists to take control of technology to ensure information integrity, advocating for them to become tech builders.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tShe warned that news organizations face imminent threats due to outdated business models and the changing landscape influenced by AI, which reduces the need for users to visit original news sources.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tRessa described the current information crisis as an 'information armageddon,' highlighting the dangers posed by technology that prioritizes profit over public good, and called for a collective effort to build alternative infrastructures for journalism.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\tThis is AI-generated. Read the article for full context. Report any errors.\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\nNobel Peace Prize laureate Maria Ressa urged journalists to take control of the technology that increasingly determines how news reaches the public in the face of artificial intelligence.\n\n\n\n“The only way you have information integrity is for journalists to become tech builders,” Ressa, Rappler’s chief executive officer, said during the Nobel Peace Conference 2026 held last September 3. “We must build, we own our data, we own the technology.”\n\n\n\nThe conference brought together Nobel Peace laureates and other participants to discuss threats facing democracy, lessons from civic movements around the world, and how the international community can support those fighting for it. This year’s discussions also included the growing influence of big tech on democratic societies.\n\n\n\t\n\t\n\t\n\n\t\n\n\nRessa warned that news organizations could “die in the next five to eight months” because the existing business model is no longer viable. Adapting to new tools is no longer enough. News organizations also need a say in the systems that determine how their journalism reaches their audience.\n\t\n\t\n\n\nFor years, publishers have relied on Google and other social media platforms to drive readers to their websites. AI-powered search is now changing that relationship by giving users direct answers to their questions.\n\n\n\nThis results in a reduced need to visit the original source. Fewer referrals mean fewer opportunities for publishers to build audiences and generate revenue from their journalism.\n\n\n\nRessa pointed to Rappler’s experience as an example of what she means by building alternatives. After years of dealing with online attacks, Rappler developed an application using the Matrix protocol, a project eventually expanded to other media groups.\n\n\n\nThe project gave news organizations infrastructure they could control, an approach journalism should take on in the AI era.\n\n\n\t\n\t\t\n\t\n“Build your own technology,” she said. “Build your multilateral organization.”\n\n\n\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t \t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t \t\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\n\n\nAn information crisis\n\n\n\nRessa described the wider problem as an “information armageddon.” The erosion of shared facts makes it harder for societies to establish a common understanding of reality.\n\n\n\nShe outlined three waves of technology that have transformed the information environment. These include social media, large language models and generative AI, and increasingly autonomous AI agents.\n\n\n\n“Every one of these stages are built to hack our biology,” Ressa said. “They’re designed not for you, they’re designed for profit.”\n\n\n\nSocial media is designed to keep people engaged, she pointed out. Chatbots can simulate human intimacy, while AI agents are increasingly capable of carrying out tasks on a user’s behalf.\n\n\n\nShe also linked technology dependence to the spread of information operations, in which false narratives can be seeded and amplified during crises, and eventually accepted as fact.\n\n\n\nFor Ressa, defending democracy now means confronting not only the information being produced, but also the systems that determine how it moves.\n\n\n\n“The technology is ruled by people who you didn’t elect, by people who have no guardrails, by people who are driven to keep you scrolling, even to your detriment,” she said.\n\n\n\nRessa co-chairs the UN’s Independent International Scientific Panel on Artificial Intelligence, a 40-member body tasked with assessing the risks, capabilities, and broader impacts of AI.\n\n\n\nThe panel warned in its July preliminary report that AI is advancing faster than governments’ ability to understand, measure and govern the technology. It said the rapid deployment of AI brings risks not only to societies and economies, but also to human rights and security.\n\n\n\nThe panel also identified AI’s effects on democracy and the information ecosystem as a major concern, warning that the technology can erode shared reality and create risks to information integrity and civic participation.\n\n\n\nIt said these risks could ultimately undermine society’s ability to benefit from AI if they are not addressed. – Rappler.com\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\tHow does this make you feel?\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tLoading","hash":"1e3a6a9120a3cf9273a81dee3a5b788612bdeb5e8fe4abb953fa73cb531cd5f7","family":"bing.com","sourceClass":"unclassified","attribution":"unknown","visibility":"public","extractionCoverage":"unattempted","completeness":"full","access":"retrieved","acquisition":"html-reader","outboundUrls":["https://www.rappler.com/technology/","https://www.rappler.com/technology/maria-ressa-journalists-protect-information-integrity-nobel-peace-conference-2026/#","https://www.rappler.com/latest/","https://www.rappler.com/video/","https://communities.rappler.com/ytUTSmRbUiTCeeZEWA?mode=download-app","https://www.rappler.com/nation/","https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/investigative/","https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/inside-track/","https://www.rappler.com/newsbreak/fact-check/","https://www.rappler.com/people/","https://www.rappler.com/voices/","https://www.rappler.com/business/","https://www.rappler.com/environment/","https://www.rappler.com/philippines/weather/","https://www.rappler.com/science/","https://www.rappler.com/world/","https://www.rappler.com/brandrap/","https://www.rappler.com/topic/special-coverages/","https://www.rappler.com/plus-membership-program/","https://www.rappler.com/newsletters","https://learn.rappler.com/","https://www.rappler.com/moveph/","https://www.rappler.com/","https://www.rappler.com/bulletin-board/","https://www.rappler.com/topics","https://communities.rappler.com/ytUTSmRbUiTCeeZEWA/whFqWiaKhaWMOwujhd/chat","https://www.rappler.com/about/","https://www.rappler.com/video/watch-now-live/","https://www.rappler.com/video/daily-wrap/","https://www.rappler.com/video/top-5-news-this-week/","https://www.rappler.com/topic/video-recap/","https://www.rappler.com/video/rappler-talk-video/","https://www.rappler.com/video/rappler-live-jam/","https://www.rappler.com/video/doc